            <div xml:id="d543">

                <pb facs="0585" n="481" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg481"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.516/226: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Culbertson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-08-27T13:00:00-05:00">August 27, 1932—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 2:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>190. With reference to the Department’s telegram 72, August 26, 6 p.m., the Under
                    Secretary for Foreign Affairs stated that the reply in question refers to
                    article 10 section 10 of the Constitution and to book 4 title 16 of the Code of
                    Civil Procedure.</p>
                <p>May I have as soon as possible instructions which would [enable?] me to make
                    clear to the present Chilean regime our attitude toward recognition? I have not
                    connected my requests for clarification of its policy toward international
                    obligations with recognition, but the implication follows from the circumstances
                    that we intend to recognize if the assurances requested are given. If you desire
                    any further data on the present situation I shall be glad to supply it.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Culbertson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
